# IAM (Identity Access Management)
### Why
Basically this is just like *Active Directory* in Windows Server to handle users easily in enterprise architecture.**IAM** 
basically create users and handle and monitor easily.  
### What
In **IAM** we have can create *Users*, *Groups*, *Roles* etc., and also maintain *Permission Boundaries/SCPs* basically 
segregating each user type in groups and monitor everything without much cluster or hassle free maintenance
### How
Once we get **Root Account** access which has no restrictions has all access where you can monitor everything and have access over all 
and not have any restrictions on anything, including deleting account so it is suggested that to create a *IAM User* with *Administrative Access* 
so if you even lose access you have root account to recover.
